# Phusion Passenger Configuration for Netcup/Plesk Webhosting
#
# IMPORTANT — two separate roots, do not point Document Root here:
#
#   Application Root (this directory) - contains package.json, app.js,
#   node_modules/ and the build/ output. Passenger cds into this directory
#   and runs app.js from it.
#
#   Document Root - must be build/client/ (a subdirectory OF this
#   directory), NOT this directory itself. Apache serves any physically
#   existing file under Document Root directly, bypassing Passenger/Node
#   entirely ("static assets are accelerated" - see
#   https://www.phusionpassenger.com/library/admin/apache/troubleshooting/nodejs/
#   and the canonical Node.js deploy layout at
#   https://www.phusionpassenger.com/library/deploy/apache/deploy/nodejs/,
#   both of which set DocumentRoot to the app's public subdirectory while
#   Passenger's application root stays one level up). If Document Root were
#   set to this directory, requests like GET /package.json, GET /.git/config
#   or GET /src/lib/db/index.ts would be served as plain static files -
#   publishing source code and internals to every visitor. Full setup steps:
#   see DEPLOYMENT.md.
#
# In Plesk's Node.js panel these are two distinct fields ("Application Root"
# and "Document Root") rather than something configured through this file -
# Plesk did not, as of this writing, warn if both are left identical
# (https://talk.plesk.com/threads/add-warning-when-document-root-equals-application-root-in-node-js-apps.391992/),
# so this is a real, easy-to-hit misconfiguration, not a hypothetical one.
#
# Adjust the Node.js path to match your server's installation:
# find it via `which node` over SSH.
#
# Startup file is app.cjs, not app.js: Passenger's Node.js loader loads it
# via require(), which cannot load an ES module ("type": "module" in
# package.json makes plain .js files ESM) - see app.cjs's own comment for
# the full explanation and citation.
# PassengerNodejs /usr/bin/node
PassengerAppType node
PassengerStartupFile app.cjs
